  plan-1 v3 plan [kept] {"says":"**Session 1: the seal (lands alone), as built in PR #2421.** Witnesses are unit tests against `fake-client.js`, in `tests/unit/jira-as-asked-region.test.js`.\n\n0. **Tidy first: empty fence.** An empty fenced code block in the words now renders as the bare fence (it used to render as a blank line between fences, which the writer split), and the D1 write gate accepts an empty fence. D1 is therefore changed, not unchanged. Witness: the empty-fence regression and a codec fixed point.\n1. **One region module, `lib/providers/jira/as-asked-region.js`**, is the only module that knows both the ADF region and the `harbour.asAsked = { v: 1, words, rendered }` property. It imports the spellings from `lib/as-asked.js`. `rendered` is a SHA-256 digest of the region's rendering (compared only, never shown).\n   * **End line by digest, not by position.** The reader takes the end-paragraph candidate whose region renders to the stored digest; the writer takes the candidate whose interior renders to the words. So words that hold the end sentence as plain or `_…_` text do not close the region early. `hasMarkerLine` and `splitAsAsked` are unchanged; Linear is unaffected.\n   * Region renders to the digest: serve exact `words`. Otherwise (edited in Jira): serve the rendering behind canonical markers. No end paragraph or no heading: no block. Property with no region: ignored.\n2. Comparison is on rendered content (digest), never ADF hashes (`localId` stays kept).\n3. All four description reads in `index.js` go through `readDescription`, and **every provider-side `client.getIssue` asks for the property**, including epic/parent and write re-reads. The fake returns only requested property keys, as Jira does. Bulk search stays display-only. Witness: a grep test that no description read bypasses the reader (comment bodies excepted).\n4. `updateIssue` writes the property in the same PUT whenever the description holds a block. Witness: byte equality across filing, later writes, `description/below` writes and human edits below the line.\n5. `descriptionRoundTrip` is a pure write-then-read through the same reader. It **throws** on a block it cannot hold (too large, or interior does not read back); `providerHoldsAsAsked` treats a throw as cannot-hold, so the seal is skipped with the existing log line. `lib/as-asked-seal.js` unchanged apart from headers. `sealForFiling`'s provider-skip branch is witnessed with a synthetic lossy round trip.\n6. **Size guard in UTF-8 bytes**: the serialized property (JSON escaping included) must be at most 32,768 bytes, the stricter reading of Atlassian's docs (bytes on some pages, characters on others). Over: seal skipped; oversized `updateIssue` returns 422 naming the size, no write.\n\n**Retires:** the \"Jira is skipped\" claims in `lib/as-asked-seal.js`, `lib/as-asked.js`, the `descriptionRoundTrip` comment, `lib/dispatch-factory.js` (\"Jira today\"), `docs/proxy-integration.md` (\"Jira files unsealed\"); the Jira \"cannot hold\" cases of the create-seal, dispatch-seal and guard tests now pin sealed and kept.\n\n**Not done here:** the live check on John's Jira (session 3, LIN-4452): whether the site keeps the digest across an ordinary editor save below the block.","paths":["lib/providers/jira/","lib/as-asked-seal.js","lib/as-asked.js","lib/dispatch-factory.js","docs/proxy-integration.md","tests/unit/jira-as-asked-region.test.js","tests/unit/as-asked-create-seal.test.js","tests/unit/as-asked-dispatch-seal.test.js","tests/unit/as-asked-readers.test.js","tests/unit/as-asked-guard.test.js"],"order":1}
