  decision-1 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Size: just the task. Cuts: none.","why":"Jira gains issue creation and a description that keeps the person's words byte-exact through later writes, which opens all three filing lanes; the Surveys' converter, rich-content and capability findings are nearby but none binds on this ask. Decided by: the standing policy."}
  decision-2 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Shape B: the exact words live in a Jira issue entity property `harbour.asAsked = { v: 1, words, rendered }`; the description holds an ordinary block (an \"As asked\" level-2 heading, the words converted by `markdownToAdf`, and the italic end line). On read, when the region still renders to `rendered`, the provider serves the exact `words`; when it doesn't, it serves what the region now renders (Linear parity).","why":"B is the only shape that is exact for agents *and* leaves Jira looking like Jira for its people. The second point decides it, because the first-dispatch seal will touch tickets the team wrote themselves. A (a code-block carrier) is a close second and cheaper: every ticket a human wrote in Jira would become a grey raw-Markdown box. C (a normalised baseline) is cheapest, but the stored words would no longer be what the person typed, and it needs a ruling from John; A and B don't, so no ruling is asked for. A lossless codec is ruled out: ADF marks accept no custom attributes, and Jira validates them. What would change it: John's site refuses `properties` in create/edit bodies (then a separate `PUT /issue/{key}/properties/harbour.asAsked`, still B); John wants the ask shown verbatim as code (then A); John rules render-exact acceptable (then C is cheaper)."}
  decision-3 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Session fit: needs multiple sessions.","why":"Session 1 holds the region reader's edge cases and the property semantics across client, fake, four reads and the writer; it lands and is verified alone, and when it lands the first-dispatch seal begins sealing Jira-native tickets on John's site. Session 2 holds createmeta, three lanes, the UI flip, and retirements across six docs and prompt files with a prompt-budget re-measure. Session 3 needs a credential no build session has by default. Split at those seams. Each piece lands and verifies on its own, and sessions 2 and 3 are each blocked by the one before."}
  decision-4 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"plan-review due: yes","why":"more than one session, writes to a real Jira in use, and it changes the sealing contract on a path that rewrites human-authored tickets."}
  verdict-1 v1 verdict [stands] STALE: plan-1@2 now at v3 {"of":"plan-review","outcome":"approve","reasoning":"Verdict: Approve. The plan's premises check out at HEAD, it opens all three filing lanes with one provider method plus an honest round trip, and its safeguards hold. The notes are corrections the builder can make without changing the plan's shape. For this piece: the seal module needs no change (`providerHoldsAsAsked`, `lib/as-asked-seal.js:57`, is a synchronous `checkAsAsked(sealed, descriptionRoundTrip(sealed))`, so a pure write-then-read round trip that includes the property turns sealing on); the proxy guard (`routes/proxy-writes.js:336/534`) compares the live `issueDescription` with the new body, so serving exact `words` from the reader is all the guard needs; the D1 premise holds (13 bodies through `adfHasUnrenderableContent(markdownToAdf(sealAsAsked(x)))`, none trips D1; the codec reached a fixed point after one pass on every sample); landing session 1 alone on a live site is safe (a failed first-dispatch seal is caught and logged, `dispatch-factory.js:1066-1079`, and only never-dispatched issues are sealed). Notes 2, 3 and 6 are folded into this version."}
  verdict-2 v1 verdict [stands] {"of":"plan-review","outcome":"approve","reasoning":"Verdict: Approve. The plan's premises check out at HEAD, it opens all three filing lanes with one provider method plus an honest round trip, and its safeguards hold. The notes are corrections the builder can make without changing the plan's shape. For this piece: every lane ends in `provider.createIssue` after `sealForFiling` (proxy create `proxy-writes.js:445/470`, session `workspace-api-issue-write.js:792`, feedback `workspace-api-feedback.js:511`). Staleness: `git log --since=2026-11-13T12:08Z` is empty for the paths involved at `4b5001ddf` = origin/main. Notes 4 (the Periodical report gate never fires on Jira) and 5 (`parentId` is not reachable through the lanes as planned) are folded into this version."}
  verdict-3 v1 verdict [stands] {"of":"plan-review","outcome":"approve","reasoning":"Verdict: Approve. The plan's premises check out at HEAD, it opens all three filing lanes with one provider method plus an honest round trip, and its safeguards hold. The notes are corrections the builder can make without changing the plan's shape. For this piece: against what was asked, the plan delivers every clause with no narrowing; if the builder has no credential for John's site, session 3 is a handoff to John, not a cut. Open questions resolved: the property limit is 32768 characters per Atlassian's entity-property docs (the builder confirms it); whether John's site accepts `properties` in create and edit bodies, its createmeta, and its required fields stay with session 3's live check, with the separate-PUT fallback and the naming 422 already planned. Note 1 (make the live check able to fail) is folded into this version."}
  decision-5 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Plan-review due: no.","why":"Covered by LIN-4445's approving plan-review (comment 61e84035-1df2-459f-8def-5afdc7001e64, rev 1). Grounding commit `4b5001ddf` (origin/main when the plan was written and reviewed; re-checked at breakdown on 2026-11-13, with no commits on the plan's paths since)."}
  decision-6 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Session fit: fits one session.","why":"Session 1 holds the region reader's edge cases and the property semantics across client, fake, the four reads and the writer, and it lands and verifies alone."}
  decision-11 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Size: a small tidy. Cuts: none.","why":"Jira holds the person's words byte-exact (plan-1 session 1, the seal), plus one tidy done first: an empty fenced code block in the words must no longer file a description that refuses every later write below the line, which is the write the seal exists to allow. Decided by: the standing policy; the system holds no setting record for that policy, so this decision rests on nothing it can link."}
  decision-12 v2 decision [stands] {"answer":"Neither offered option. The end line is chosen by digest, not by position: the reader takes the end-paragraph candidate whose region renders to the stored rendered digest; the writer takes the candidate whose interior renders to the words. hasMarkerLine and splitAsAsked stay unchanged, and descriptionRoundTrip runs the same reader.","why":"Leaving hasMarkerLine and splitAsAsked unchanged keeps Linear unaffected. The digest already tells kept from edited, so it can also pick the end candidate, which removes the silent early close. Witnessed in tests/unit/jira-as-asked-region.test.js (PR #2421)."}
  verdict-4 v1 verdict [stands] {"of":"review","outcome":"approve-with-notes","reasoning":"PR #2421 at head f9fa4a80ca19503bebbf5abd81dc83d3e86296b6 (base 4b5001ddf; merges cleanly onto origin/main cfd152fa4, which touched none of its 22 files). CI green on that head; seal/codec suites re-run locally green. Every item of the plan piece is met: one region module importing the as-asked spellings; every description read through readDescription and all 9 provider getIssue calls pass AS_ASKED_READ; updateIssue writes the property in the same PUT; descriptionRoundTrip pure via holdAsAsked+readDescription; UTF-8 byte size guard at 32,768 with 422 and no write; client/fake property shape. Departures (end line by digest, bytes, throwing round trip, empty-fence tidy, every getIssue asks) are recorded on LIN-4445. The empty-fence change is a codec-shape rule, not a rich-content rule, so D1 rich-content guard unchanged holds in substance. Extras declared: isHermeticTestToken consolidation (behaviour-identical) and docs/areas/providers.md. No regressions to codec (LIN-3556..3570) or seal (LIN-4352/4361/4362) work. Ten mutations each went red; 16 extra shapes held byte-exact. Class is Jira-only; createIssue is LIN-4451 scope. Notes: one close-out test fix; three live checks are post-deploy (LIN-4452), not merge conditions. Rollback: revert the squash commit; written properties are ignored and sealed descriptions fall back to the unsealed readers, no data loss. Merging unblocks LIN-4451."}
